# Shell scripts MUST keep Unix (LF) line endings, on every platform.
#
# Git for Windows installs with core.autocrlf=true by default — which is what
# `winget install --id Git.Git -e` gives a student, since a silent install
# takes the installer's defaults. With autocrlf=true and no .gitattributes,
# cloning this repo on Windows rewrites check_setup.sh with CRLF endings, and
# Git Bash then fails on the carriage returns before running a single check:
#
#     check_setup.sh: line 2: $'\r': command not found
#     check_setup.sh: line 12: syntax error near unexpected token `$'{\r''
#
# That is the last step of laptop setup, so it would greet every Windows
# student with what looks like a broken script. `eol=lf` pins the checkout.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Keep the Python sources and the lock file LF too, so a diff never shows a
# whole-file change just because it was edited on a different platform.
*.py text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
uv.lock text eol=lf
